        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>Air Leakage Areas:



Around Attic Hatch



Bathroom Fan is outdated and allowing cold air to enter the home.



Windows are showing quite a bit of air leakage around glass/vinly intersection



Utilidor showing quite a bit of air leakage.



Air duct in living room bringing cold air into the house. Check with a ventilation specialist. This may have been an old supply for combustion air for older furnace and hot water heater. 



</airSeal>
                <windows>Upgrade windows to energy star zone 3 rated



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on windows/doors and the criteria for these products.

</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>If there are air sealing upgrades completed to the home, it will make it tighter, but it will not allow for the home to breathe as it was before. A mechanical ventilation system such as a HRV/ERV should be installed to allow the home to be properly ventilated. Not installing a system after air sealing can lead to issues with mold and mildew. It will also help increase air quality in the home as well.



The HRV/ERV will recovery heated exhaust air via a heat exchanger and preheat supply air with the extracted heat, making the units very efficient. 



HRV = Heat Recovery Ventilator

ERV = Enthalpy Recovery Ventilator



ERV also can control indoor humidity levels as well.



Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on ventilation systems and the criteria for these products.

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>See heating system upgrades.</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>Install a digital or smart thermostat to help reduce oil consumption.



Option 1:

As the furnace gets older and near the end of its life, you may want to take a look at a few different options. If you are planning on staying with oil heating, I would recommend you upgrade to and oil boiler that is ENERGY STAR® rated with an AFUE of 87%. This would still allow for hot water heating in the boiler as well. This system would be direct vented through a side wall and would not require the chimney to be used any more. This could allow for the chimey to be sealed up and would reduce the amount of air leakage into the home. Savings associated with this upgrade would be 22 GJ/year. 



Option 2:

Another option would be to switch to gas/propane for heating and hot water. If you were to do this, I would recommend a Combi Boiler/DHW system. This would combine your heating and hot water into one system. Make sure to look for one that is ENERGY STAR® and has at least a 95% AFUE rating. Savings associated with this upgrade would be 55 GJ/year. 





Please refer to Artic Energy Alliance for rebates on Heating/DHW upgrades and the criteria for these products.</HeatingSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
